      "id": "56973a6d-338a-4796-989a-7287e490d6c6",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "1. Place value and unsigned binary",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "16b5beda-31b0-4447-a1ab-9ceeb8237f7d",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "80af8d17-712a-4141-bd67-73f2e43bbc96",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "042f18f4-fc9e-4f17-a0d8-f468946ed3b7",
      "text": "A **bit** is one binary digit: 0 or 1. A bit pattern gets its numerical meaning from its base, bit width, and encoding.\n\n- **MSB:** the leftmost, highest-weight bit.\n- **LSB:** the rightmost, lowest-weight bit.\n- **Nibble:** 4 bits. **Byte:** 8 bits. These first examples are unsigned.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "7aeba6d6-26bb-46de-a908-a730d2d2b619",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "912bbdff-05e7-4f95-ba91-fd18de50dba5",
      "text": "In base $k$, every digit is between $0$ and $k-1$. Moving one position left multiplies its weight by $k$; moving right across the radix point divides by $k$.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "display": true,
      "id": "e4b8e21e-9825-44cd-8172-0868d5046f26",
      "latex": "V=\\sum_{i=-m}^{n-1}b_i k^i",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "3f080c3b-0089-4e2b-9158-bc7a5a2da3a6",
      "text": "$b_i$ is the digit at position $i$; $n$ digits lie before the radix point and $m$ after it. The units digit has index 0.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Position",
        "3",
        "2",
        "1",
        "0",
        "-1",
        "-2",
        "-3"
      ],
      "id": "8c6e1447-2c9f-4848-ae36-47775be2e57e",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Binary weight",
          "8",
          "4",
          "2",
          "1",
          "1/2",
          "1/4",
          "1/8"
        ],
        [
          "Example digit",
          "1",
          "0",
          "1",
          "0",
          "0",
          "1",
          "0"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$(1010.01)_2=(10.25)_{10}$.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "62257eba-ae8e-437f-869d-8d53184bdef0",
      "prompt": "Convert $(1010.01)_2$ to decimal.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Only the 1 bits contribute.",
          "latex": "V=2^3+2^1+2^{-2}"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Evaluate the weights.",
          "latex": "V=8+2+\\tfrac14=10.25"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Lecture example: a binary fraction",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "2d159f2e-c624-4c0d-932f-c8b210505bb7",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "df62543a-fee1-4f51-883d-77d7811aed33",
      "text": "An unsigned $n$-bit integer has $2^n$ distinct patterns, representing $0$ through $2^n-1$. Leading zeroes preserve the value but may specify the required width.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "11 bits. Count the full range, including zero.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "1ab95c1e-c943-4b45-bed8-846c282f9eb8",
          "source_id": "93f0739f-31e6-41f1-8f6f-b09623073bb0",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "3506150a-244b-4472-87f1-837805ad1c8a",
      "prompt": "How many bits are needed to represent decimal 1029 as an unsigned integer?",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Ten bits represent only 0 through 1023.",
          "latex": "2^{10}-1=1023<1029"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Eleven bits include the required value.",
          "latex": "1029<2^{11}=2048"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Tutorial 2: choosing a width",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$6.625$.",
      "citations": [],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "928a06a0-1fda-4be7-a4aa-81e7caf70572",
      "origin": "original",
      "prompt": "Find the decimal value of $(110.101)_2$.",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Use negative exponents to the right of the point.",
          "latex": "4+2+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac18=6.625"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: decode the radix point",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "e4d5eedc-42c7-4b4c-9b69-bce4fa238622",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "1b708a29-a4a4-469f-abf1-9f7fe47e89bb",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ace0f79d-46e1-4d42-b131-c8cab27b4d20",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "687c3afe-9856-467e-a6f2-19b7daa12bab",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "281ac313-67f9-483c-80d2-1f928fdd7d82",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "2. Decimal \u2194 binary, including fractions",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "e4d5eedc-42c7-4b4c-9b69-bce4fa238622",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "1b708a29-a4a4-469f-abf1-9f7fe47e89bb",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ace0f79d-46e1-4d42-b131-c8cab27b4d20",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "fa4e9057-0f83-4b57-822e-23583c875a47",
      "text": "Convert the two sides of the radix point separately.\n\n- **Integer:** repeatedly divide by the target base; read the remainders from bottom to top.\n- **Fraction:** repeatedly multiply by the target base; take each integer part in top-to-bottom order and continue with the remaining fraction.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$(23.375)_{10}=(10111.011)_2$.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "1b708a29-a4a4-469f-abf1-9f7fe47e89bb",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ace0f79d-46e1-4d42-b131-c8cab27b4d20",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "c6608496-ded7-4e72-874a-73589e833fd4",
      "prompt": "Convert $(23.375)_{10}$ to binary.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Divide the integer part by 2. The remainder is the next bit from the right.",
          "latex": "23=2(11)+1,\\quad11=2(5)+1"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Continue until the quotient is zero.",
          "latex": "5=2(2)+1,\\quad2=2(1)+0,\\quad1=2(0)+1"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Read the remainders upward.",
          "latex": "23=(10111)_2"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Multiply the fraction by 2; save each integer part.",
          "latex": "0.375(2)=0.75,\\quad0.75(2)=1.5"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Continue using only the remaining fractional part.",
          "latex": "0.5(2)=1.0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad0.375=(.011)_2"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Lecture example: $23.375$ to binary",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "e4d5eedc-42c7-4b4c-9b69-bce4fa238622",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ace0f79d-46e1-4d42-b131-c8cab27b4d20",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "33655c73-5aa0-4d91-8d22-0a4022d0e23a",
      "text": "The integer remainders arrive LSB first because each division removes the lowest-weight digit. Fractional multiplication reveals the highest-weight fractional digit first.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "687c3afe-9856-467e-a6f2-19b7daa12bab",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Fraction remaining",
        "Multiply by 2",
        "Next bit"
      ],
      "id": "327ee1b5-fc58-42ef-bd78-4ee3a2bdcd8e",
      "rows": [
        [
          "0.2",
          "0.4",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0.4",
          "0.8",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0.8",
          "1.6",
          "1"
        ],
        [
          "0.6",
          "1.2",
          "1"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "687c3afe-9856-467e-a6f2-19b7daa12bab",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "0c861aa2-bed8-4bcb-8a4d-1c9f4c9f8895",
      "text": "For $0.2$, the remaining fraction returns to $0.2$, so the bits repeat: $(0.2)_{10}=(.\\overline{0011})_2$. A reduced fraction terminates in binary only when its denominator is a power of two.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$0.625$, which is below $2/3$ by $1/24$. Truncation and rounding are different instructions.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "5ce2c8c3-a343-493d-834e-53c179d1c15c",
          "source_id": "bf2ca805-96f9-49d4-87da-ba6471d6a531",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "31a9a53a-127e-454a-b051-a5a9de4a2ea3",
      "prompt": "Approximate $2/3$ with four binary fractional bits, truncating after the fourth bit. Find the absolute error.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Repeated doubling gives the repeating pattern 10.",
          "latex": "\\tfrac23=(.101010\\ldots)_2"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Keep four fractional bits.",
          "latex": "(.1010)_2=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac18=\\tfrac58"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Subtract the approximation from the exact value.",
          "latex": "\\tfrac23-\\tfrac58=\\tfrac1{24}\\approx0.0416667"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Past midterm: limited precision",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "2b60a992-63c3-46fa-b8f3-20a3023605dc",
      "text": "With $m$ binary fractional bits, the spacing is $2^{-m}$. For a nonnegative value, truncation has error less than $2^{-m}$; rounding to nearest has error at most $2^{-(m+1)}$, assuming the rounded value fits.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "No; four-bit truncation is $.0010_2=0.125$, error $0.025$.",
      "citations": [],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "3615c490-8168-4702-b50d-739382e96055",
      "origin": "original",
      "prompt": "Does $3/20$ terminate in binary? Find its first four fractional bits and the truncation error.",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "The reduced denominator 20 contains a factor of 5, so the binary expansion repeats.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Doubling gives integer parts 0, 0, 1, 0.",
          "latex": "(.0010)_2=\\tfrac18"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Compare exact fractions.",
          "latex": "\\tfrac3{20}-\\tfrac18=\\tfrac1{40}=0.025"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: detect a repeating expansion",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "7e4c9ea0-9451-4ac0-8b7e-6f03ba35783a",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
